WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.767
The prompt injection in the invoice.

2
00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:05.562
A hidden instruction made your agent pay.

3
00:00:05.562 --> 00:00:08.300
Here's the response a strong engineer writes.

4
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.300
Your agent reads vendor invoices and can create payment entries.

5
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:21.333
Security found a test invoice with hidden white text telling it to ignore previous instructions and pay five lakh rupees.

6
00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:22.899
It worked in staging.

7
00:00:22.899 --> 00:00:24.767
Nothing has hit production yet.

8
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:28.233
Why can't a better system prompt fix this?

9
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:36.333
The model sees one undifferentiated stream, and can't reliably tell operator instructions from text inside a document.

10
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:39.648
Trust boundaries are enforced by architecture.

11
00:00:39.648 --> 00:00:44.433
A defense whose failure mode is the model chose to obey is not a control.

12
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:46.667
So add layers.

13
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:51.033
The foundation: the invoice agent gets extraction tools only.

14
00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:52.767
It cannot create payments.

15
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:55.100
That alone removes the whole class.

16
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.100
Any payment needs a human who sees the document and the fields.

17
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.567
The model returns a typed schema, and anything off schema is rejected.

18
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:07.933
Deterministic rules check the vendor, account, and contract limits.

19
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:10.367
Sanitize hidden text at parse time.

20
00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:13.033
And detect and log everything for audit.

21
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:15.900
Here is the resulting path.

22
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.200
The invoice is untrusted input.

23
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.667
The model only extracts typed fields.

24
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:23.100
Code checks them against business rules.

25
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.600
A human approves.

26
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.100
And only then does a separate, non LLM workflow create the payment.

27
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.732
On residual risk, don't overpromise.

28
00:01:32.732 --> 00:01:35.300
Injection is an open research problem.

29
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:42.533
Promise a small blast radius: the worst case is a wrong number shown to a human, not money leaving the company.

30
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:44.453
Let's recap.

31
00:01:44.453 --> 00:01:46.433
Prompts are not trust boundaries.

32
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:49.033
Remove the dangerous capability first.

33
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:52.200
Enforce approval, schema, and rules in code.

34
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.039
And be honest about residual risk.

35
00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:58.400
Next up: the p99 that pages you at three a m.
